(6501300, 966514, 109341, '<td valign="top">Great shot! One of my favorites!</td>', 'Great shot! One of my favorites!', '2011-08-10 23:46:18.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501400, 966345, 109341, '<td valign="top">Good portrait, but just don''t get the Noir feel from this.  -1 for unlit cigarette.   Lighting is ok but I find the white had and white tie too distracting and rather conflicting with the noir genre (if that makes sense).  Still solid effort. 6</td>', 'Good portrait, but just don''t get the Noir feel from this.  -1 for unlit cigarette.   Lighting is ok but I find the white had and white tie too distracting and rather conflicting with the noir genre (if that makes sense).  Still solid effort. 6', '2011-08-11 02:24:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501412, 966245, 109341, '<td valign="top">I came away extremely conflicted from this image. On the positive side, perhaps no other shot here (with exception of one) has made me come back to it as many times and stop and think about it.  The model is just superb!  She did a fantastic job with the facial expression, posture, etc.  It just screams Hitchcock to me!!!  The lighting is almost great....I just think it''s a touch too harsh as the completely washed out areas on her face and forearm indicate.  I also like the processing with a slightly greenish tint.  However, here is why I''m giving this the score that I am:<br/> <br/>1. Technical aspects (not sharp enough, high-key areas are over-exposed...there are also two distracting white spots on the black part of the blouse (either hot pixels or ???)..should have been cloned out.  More of the knife shadow in the composition would have been great!<br/> <br/>2. As much as I love Hitchcock and horror genre, in my classical definition of Film Noir this just DNMC.  I wish it did...and perhaps it''s my own film snobbery but I simply cannot vote this higher in a Film Noir challenge.<br/> <br/>With better technical aspects this would have been a 7-8 shot from me in this challenge.  The way it stands...it''s a 6...although part of me really wishes I could score this higher.  It''s a superb shot on it''s own.</td>', 'I came away extremely conflicted from this image. On the positive side, perhaps no other shot here (with exception of one) has made me come back to it as many times and stop and think about it.  The model is just superb!  She did a fantastic job with the facial expression, posture, etc.  It just screams Hitchcock to me!!!  The lighting is almost great....I just think it''s a touch too harsh as the completely washed out areas on her face and forearm indicate.  I also like the processing with a slightly greenish tint.  However, here is why I''m giving this the score that I am: 1. Technical aspects (not sharp enough, high-key areas are over-exposed...there are also two distracting white spots on the black part of the blouse (either hot pixels or ???)..should have been cloned out.  More of the knife shadow in the composition would have been great! 2. As much as I love Hitchcock and horror genre, in my classical definition of Film Noir this just DNMC.  I wish it did...and perhaps it''s my own film snobbery but I simply cannot vote this higher in a Film Noir challenge. With better technical aspects this would have been a 7-8 shot from me in this challenge.  The way it stands...it''s a 6...although part of me really wishes I could score this higher.  It''s a superb shot on it''s own.', '2011-08-11 02:41:15.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501413, 965393, 109341, '<td valign="top">I like this shot primarily because of the composition and the props used. With the more dramatic lighting and more natural position of the gun this may have been an 8-9 from me.  Nonetheless great job! 7</td>', 'I like this shot primarily because of the composition and the props used. With the more dramatic lighting and more natural position of the gun this may have been an 8-9 from me.  Nonetheless great job! 7', '2011-08-11 02:44:33.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501461, 966335, 117037, '<td valign="top">Beautiful...my only 9.</td>', 'Beautiful...my only 9.', '2011-08-11 06:25:16.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501467, 966435, 131284, '<td valign="top">The lighting, the outfit, the brick work; all say classic film noir to me, so you''ve done a great job on the scene! The next questions is (which for film noir I believe to be the most important one); is the story/emotion there?<br/> <br/>For me it is to a point. The way the shadows fall on the protagonist really add to the atmosphere as does his stance, and the space left in the lighting for a second figure to emerge (as suggested by the title) is a great touch too. I''m left wondering what the purpose of this meeting is, where in the story our protagonist is etc.<br/> <br/>My criticism with this scene, which may seem minor but I believe affects the image a lot, would have to be that I find the white static on the ground (which I guess is grass) very distracting and a more even/darker tone such as asphalt I feel would lend itself much better to the scene.<br/> <br/>The white flecks in the brickwork I also find distracting, and perhaps a softer focus would have minimised those (which being film noir I don''t believe would negatively affect the image in any way) - in fact I believe a softer focus may enhance this picture slightly, allowing the foliage to blend in more with the rest of the scene and leaving the scene slightly more open to interpretation by the viewer, which imo is a great way of increasing the atmosphere (just looking through the images I''ve rated along side this one, most others are in softer focus than this).<br/> <br/>Anyway, I gave this a 7, it meets the criteria well and is a good image regardless. Hope other voters are enjoying it as much as I am, and of course what I''ve criticised in this scene may be something another voter likes about it =).</td>', 'The lighting, the outfit, the brick work; all say classic film noir to me, so you''ve done a great job on the scene! The next questions is (which for film noir I believe to be the most important one); is the story/emotion there? For me it is to a point. The way the shadows fall on the protagonist really add to the atmosphere as does his stance, and the space left in the lighting for a second figure to emerge (as suggested by the title) is a great touch too. I''m left wondering what the purpose of this meeting is, where in the story our protagonist is etc. My criticism with this scene, which may seem minor but I believe affects the image a lot, would have to be that I find the white static on the ground (which I guess is grass) very distracting and a more even/darker tone such as asphalt I feel would lend itself much better to the scene. The white flecks in the brickwork I also find distracting, and perhaps a softer focus would have minimised those (which being film noir I don''t believe would negatively affect the image in any way) - in fact I believe a softer focus may enhance this picture slightly, allowing the foliage to blend in more with the rest of the scene and leaving the scene slightly more open to interpretation by the viewer, which imo is a great way of increasing the atmosphere (just looking through the images I''ve rated along side this one, most others are in softer focus than this). Anyway, I gave this a 7, it meets the criteria well and is a good image regardless. Hope other voters are enjoying it as much as I am, and of course what I''ve criticised in this scene may be something another voter likes about it =).', '2011-08-11 06:48:48.000000', NULL, 1),
(6501475, 966483, 114233, '<td valign="top"><b>Composition wise </b>- Its pretty good but may have benefitted more by having the vanishing point on a thirds line and not in the middle of the picture but that is a minor point. The far left side seems pretty empty compared to the busy right hand side and feels slightly unbalanced.<br/><b>Technique</b> - good use of slow shutter speed. Some items are sharp and clear and others are blury drawing the eye in down the length of the train and under the bridge.<br/><b>PP </b>- The vignette in the 3 corners adds interest and I am wondering if a vignette in the top left could have added to the picture? The slightly sepia tone is nice for this image but I wonder if the voters wanted a pure B&amp;W shot to fit the challenge theme?<br/><b>Overall </b>- I didnt take the challenge description too literally but I suspect that there isnt enough of a "human" factor (protagonist dies, etc as per challenge description) in this image to make it film noir enough for the average voter. I like this shot and would probably have given it a 6 (I''m not voting). Hope that helps!</td>', 'Composition wise - Its pretty good but may have benefitted more by having the vanishing point on a thirds line and not in the middle of the picture but that is a minor point. The far left side seems pretty empty compared to the busy right hand side and feels slightly unbalanced.Technique - good use of slow shutter speed. Some items are sharp and clear and others are blury drawing the eye in down the length of the train and under the bridge.PP - The vignette in the 3 corners adds interest and I am wondering if a vignette in the top left could have added to the picture? The slightly sepia tone is nice for this image but I wonder if the voters wanted a pure B&W shot to fit the challenge theme?Overall - I didnt take the challenge description too literally but I suspect that there isnt enough of a "human" factor (protagonist dies, etc as per challenge description) in this image to make it film noir enough for the average voter. I like this shot and would probably have given it a 6 (I''m not voting). Hope that helps!', '2011-08-11 07:42:50.000000', NULL, 1),
